#X text 240 83 Attention! The order of connecting the strip: first
ground(!) \, then power (+5V) \, then the data pin \, this is necessary
to protect the first LED from burning out. It is recommended to install
a 300-500 Ohm resistor in the Data circuit.;
#X obj 230 88 cnv 1 3 75 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -258049 -1 0;
#X text 240 168 The disconnection procedure is strictly reverse: first
the data pin \, then the +5V power \, and then ground.;
#X obj 230 173 cnv 1 3 30 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -258049 -1 0;
#X text 240 208 When using the object \, you need to specify the number
of the SPI (@spi_bus property).;
#X obj 230 213 cnv 1 2 30 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -260289 -1 0;
#X text 240 248 The object uses an internal pixel buffer \, with the
colors of which it works by sending messages to the object. After finishing
work with the pixel buffer \, it needs to be sent to the LED strip
using a bang or the [flush( command.;
#X obj 230 253 cnv 1 2 75 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -158925 -1 0;
#X text 240 333 To specify the group of pixels we are going to work
with \, two types of ranges are used: @slice and @lslice.;
#X obj 50 436 bng 15 250 50 0 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -262144 -1
-1;
#X obj 88 436 tgl 15 0 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -262144 -1 -1 0 1;
#X msg 270 436 fill @color8 255 0 0 \, bang;
#X msg 88 465 @connect \$1;
#X msg 270 465 fill @slice 3 -3 2 @color 0 0 1 \, bang;
